[2026-03-15T20:12:29.007Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GzH27tbEG3SqujwzE91Bgx","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# app-building\n     2→\n     3→Simple and extensible platform for dark factory agentic app building: creating apps\n     4→according to a spec without human involvement along the way. Example use cases:\n     5→\n     6→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"Build me an app XYZ based on this spec: ...\"`\n     7→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"Continue maintaining app XYZ and fix these bugs: ...\"`\n     8→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -i` for interactive access to the agent.\n     9→\n    10→### Tech stack\n    11→\n    12→Every app the agent builds uses the same stack:\n    13→\n    14→* **React** — frontend UI\n    15→* **Netlify** — hosting and serverless functions (backend API)\n    16→* **Neon** — serverless Postgres database\n    17→* **Playwright** — integration tests\n    18→\n    19→If you need a different stack, this platform isn't the right fit yet.\n    20→\n    21→Core ideas:\n    22→\n    23→* The agent runs within a docker container that clones a repo and exposes an HTTP server for control.\n    24→* The agent builds by following a set of skill documents with guides\n    25→  for breaking its work down into tasks and directives for performing those tasks.\n    26→* The agent commits logs for it to review later and improve its skills.\n    27→* All code changes are committed and pushed back to the remote from inside the container.\n    28→\n    29→Containers can run locally or remotely.\n    30→\n    31→## Prerequisites\n    32→\n    33→- **Node.js 18+**\n    34→- **A GitHub repo** to build into, with a feature branch checked out (not `main`)\n    35→\n    36→## Setup\n    37→\n    38→```bash\n    39→npm install\n    40→```\n    41→\n    42→Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in the values:\n    43→\n    44→| Variable | Required | Description |\n    45→|---|---|---|\n    46→| `INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID` | Yes | Infisical machine identity Client ID |\n    47→| `INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET` | Yes | Infisical machine identity Client Secret |\n    48→| `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` | Yes | Infisical project ID |\n    49→| `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT` | Yes | Infisical environment (e.g. `prod`) |\n    50→| `FLY_API_TOKEN` | For remote | Fly.io personal access token |\n    51→| `FLY_APP_NAME` | For remote | Fly.io app name for container namespace |\n    52→| `LOCAL_CONTAINER_PORT` | No | Override the host port for local containers (auto-selected if unset) |\n    53→\n    54→Build secrets (API keys for Anthropic, GitHub, Netlify, Neon, etc.) are managed in **Infisical** under the `/global/` folder. The orchestration scripts fetch them at container startup and inject them into the container environment. See `src/package/.env.example` for the full list of required build secrets.\n    55→\n    56→Per-branch deployment secrets (database URLs, site IDs) are stored in Infisical under `/branches/<branch>/` and fetched by the container at deploy time.\n    57→\n    58→### Infisical setup\n    59→\n    60→1. Create a project at [app.infisical.com](https://app.infisical.com)\n    61→2. Create a `/global/` folder and add the required build secrets listed in `src/package/.env.example`\n    62→3. Create `/branches/<branch>/` folders as needed for per-branch deployment secrets\n    63→4. Create a machine identity with Universal Auth and add `INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID`, `INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET`, `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`, and `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT` to your `.env`\n    64→\n    65→### GitHub token\n    66→\n    67→Create a token at github.com/settings/tokens and enable the **`repo`** scope (the top-level checkbox). This gives the agent the read/write access it needs to clone your repo and push commits back. Add it to the Infisical `/global/` folder as `GITHUB_TOKEN`.\n    68→\n    69→### Branch setup\n    70→\n    71→The agent will not run when checked out on `main` — this is intentional to prevent accidental pushes to the main branch. Before running the agent, make sure you're on a feature branch:\n    72→\n    73→```bash\n    74→git checkout -b feature/my-app\n    75→git push -u origin feature/my-app\n    76→```\n    77→\n    78→The agent uses your current branch by default. Use `--branch` to override and target a different branch than the one you have checked out locally.\n    79→\n    80→---\n    81→\n    82→## Running Locally\n    83→\n    84→Runs the agent in a Docker container on your machine.\n    85→\n    86→### Additional prerequisites\n    87→\n    88→- **Docker Desktop** — must be installed and running. Download at docker.com/products/docker-desktop.\n    89→\n    90→### Usage\n    91→\n    92→```bash\n    93→npm run agent -- -p \"<prompt>\"\n    94→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"<prompt>\"\n    95→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app --push-branch feature/xyz -p \"<prompt>\"\n    96→```\n    97→\n    98→Starts a container, queues the prompt, then detaches. The container clones your repo, processes the prompt, commits and pushes results, then exits.\n    99→\n   100→Use `--push-branch` if you want to clone from one branch but push results to a different one.\n   101→\n   102→### Interactive mode\n   103→\n   104→```bash\n   105→npm run agent -- -i\n   106→```\n   107→\n   108→Chat with the agent in real time. Output is streamed via event polling. Press ESC to interrupt the current message. On exit, the container detaches and finishes any remaining work.\n   109→\n   110→---\n   111→\n   112→## Running Remotely (Fly.io)\n   113→\n   114→Runs the agent on a Fly.io machine instead of your local Docker. Useful for longer builds or running without tying up your machine.\n   115→\n   116→### Additional prerequisites\n   117→\n   118→- A **Fly.io account** at fly.io\n   119→- The **`flyctl` CLI** installed: `brew install flyctl` then `fly auth login`\n   120→\n   121→### Fly setup\n   122→\n   123→1. Create a Fly app to use as the container namespace:\n   124→   ```bash\n   125→   fly apps create app-building-agent\n   126→   ```\n   127→   Or create one via the Fly dashboard — the app name is just a namespace, no deployment needed.\n   128→\n   129→2. Add `FLY_API_TOKEN` (from fly.io/user/personal_access_tokens) and `FLY_APP_NAME` to your `.env`\n   130→\n   131→### Usage\n   132→\n   133→```bash\n   134→npm run agent -- --remote -p \"<prompt>\"\n   135→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app --remote -p \"<prompt>\"\n   136→```\n   137→\n   138→The agent provisions a `performance-cpu-16x` Fly machine (32GB RAM), clones your repo, builds the app, pushes results, and destroys the machine when done. Docker Desktop is not required.\n   139→\n   140→---\n   141→\n   142→## Monitoring & Control\n   143→\n   144→These commands work the same whether running locally or remotely.\n   145→\n   146→### Checking status\n   147→\n   148→```bash\n   149→npm run status\n   150→```\n   151→\n   152→Connects to the running container's HTTP API and shows state, revision, queue depth, cost, and recent log output. Tails logs in real-time (Ctrl+C to stop). Errors if no agent is running.\n   153→\n   154→### Stopping the agent\n   155→\n   156→```bash\n   157→npm run stop\n   158→npm run stop -- <containerName>\n   159→```\n   160→\n   161→Sends an HTTP stop signal. Without arguments, finds and stops all running containers. Pass a container name to stop a specific one.\n   162→\n   163→## Replay Integration (Optional)\n   164→\n   165→* Replay integration is optional but significantly improves the agent's ability to fix test\n   166→  failures.\n   167→\n   168→[Replay](https://replay.io) is a time-travel debugging tool. When integrated, the agent uses\n   169→Replay's Playwright browser to record test runs, then queries those recordings to understand\n   170→exactly what happened when a test failed — inspecting network requests, component state,\n   171→console errors, and step-by-step UI interactions without re-running the test.\n   172→\n   173→Without Replay, the agent only has Playwright's error output to work from, which is often\n   174→not enough to diagnose subtle failures. With Replay, it can pinpoint the root cause directly\n   175→and fix it in fewer iterations.\n   176→\n   177→### Setup\n   178→\n   179→1. Create an account at [app.replay.io](https://app.replay.io)\n   180→2. Go to your team settings and generate an API key\n   181→3. Add it to Infisical `/global/` as `RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY`\n   182→\n   183→The Replay browser is already installed in the Docker image — no additional setup needed.\n   184→Once the key is present, the agent automatically gains access to these debugging tools\n   185→during test runs:\n   186→\n   187→| Tool | What it shows |\n   188→|---|---|\n   189→| `PlaywrightSteps` | Step-by-step test actions with timing — first stop for any failure |\n   190→| `NetworkRequest` | Request payloads and response bodies for API calls |\n   191→| `Screenshot` | Visual snapshot at any point in the recording |\n   192→| `Logpoint` | Whether a specific function or event handler was called |\n   193→| `ConsoleMessages` | Browser console output at any point |\n   194→| `ReactRenders` | React component render history and props |\n   195→\n   196→## Finding Your App\n   197→\n   198→Once the agent has finished, check out the branch it pushed to. The built app lives at:\n   199→\n   200→```\n   201→apps/<AppName>/\n   202→```\n   203→\n   204→Key files:\n   205→- `apps/<AppName>/src/` — React frontend source code\n   206→- `apps/<AppName>/netlify/functions/` — serverless backend functions\n   207→- `apps/<AppName>/tests/` — Playwright integration tests\n   208→- `apps/<AppName>/AppSpec.md` — the spec the app was built from\n   209→- `apps/<AppName>/docs/tests.md` — the full test specification\n   210→\n   211→### Checking the deployment\n   212→\n   213→After a successful deploy, the app URL and resource IDs are saved to:\n   214→\n   215→```\n   216→apps/<AppName>/deployment.txt\n   217→```\n   218→\n   219→Example contents:\n   220→```\n   221→url=https://your-app-abc123.netlify.app\n   222→site_id=<netlify-site-id>\n   223→neon_project_id=<neon-project-id>\n   224→database_url=postgresql://...\n   225→deployed_at=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\n   226→```\n   227→\n   228→Open the `url` value in a browser to see the live app. Each subsequent deploy appends a history entry to this file describing what changed.\n   229→\n   230→## Skills\n   231→\n   232→The provided skill documents emphasize a structured approach for autonomously building\n   233→high quality, well tested apps from the initial spec. During the initial app build\n   234→it does the following:\n   235→\n   236→1. Designs a comprehensive test specification based on the initial spec.\n   237→2. Builds the app and writes tests to match the spec.\n   238→3. Gets all the tests to pass, deploys to production, and does further testing.\n   239→\n   240→The initial build will not come out perfect. The agent can followup with maintenance passes\n   241→where it checks to make sure it closely followed the spec and skill directives and fixes\n   242→any issues it finds. It will also fix reported bugs and update the skills to avoid\n   243→similar problems in the future.\n   244→\n   245→As long as each individual step the agent takes is within its capabilities (it can usually\n   246→do it but not always) the agent will converge on an app that follows the initial spec\n   247→and skill directives.\n   248→\n   249→### Writing a good prompt\n   250→\n   251→The quality of the initial prompt directly affects the quality of the app. A good prompt includes:\n   252→\n   253→* **What the app does** — describe the core purpose in a sentence or two\n   254→* **Key features** — list the main things users can do (e.g. create, edit, delete, filter, sort)\n   255→* **Data the app stores** — what entities exist and how they relate (e.g. users have many tasks, tasks have a status)\n   256→* **User roles** — if different users have different access levels, say so\n   257→* **Any specific UI or UX requirements** — e.g. a dashboard layout, a modal for editing, etc.\n   258→\n   259→Example of a detailed prompt:\n   260→```\n   261→Build a project management app where users can create projects and add tasks to them.\n   262→Each task has a title, description, status (todo/in-progress/done), priority (low/medium/high),\n   263→and due date. Users can filter tasks by status and priority, and sort by due date or priority.\n   264→Include a dashboard showing task counts by status across all projects.\n   265→```\n   266→\n   267→The more specific you are, the closer the first build will be to what you want.\n   268→\n   269→### Iterating on a built app\n   270→\n   271→Once the agent finishes the initial build, you can ask it to make changes by running it\n   272→again on the same branch with a new prompt describing what to fix or add:\n   273→\n   274→```bash\n   275→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"The task filter isn't working correctly — selecting 'done' still shows in-progress tasks. Also add the ability to assign tasks to team members.\"\n   276→```\n   277→\n   278→The agent will clone the branch, read the existing code and spec, make the requested changes,\n   279→run the tests, and push back. Each run is independent — you can queue as many follow-up\n   280→prompts as needed.\n   281→\n   282→Key things to watch out for:\n   283→\n   284→* Best suited for CRUD and API-calling apps up to a medium level of complexity.\n   285→  Overly complicated or specialized apps will not work as well yet.\n   286→* A full initial build typically costs $2–10 in Anthropic API usage depending on app complexity.\n   287→  Monitor spend with `npm run status` which shows running cost, and set API spend limits in your\n   288→  Anthropic console as a safeguard.\n   289→\n   290→## Branches\n   291→\n   292→The organization of branches in this repository is a little involved.\n   293→\n   294→* main: Core orchestration logic and app building skills.\n   295→* loop-builder: Source for the Loop Builder app https://loop-builder.netlify.app/\n   296→* loop-builder-base: Base branch for Loop Builder apps, has additional documentation and requirements.\n   297→* test-failure-benchmark: Source for the Test Failure Benchmark app https://test-failure-benchmark.netlify.app/\n   298→\n   299→Other branches generally have various automatically built apps of one sort or another.\n   300→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgMUt9oWE7HjVtCdVeoZvG","session_id":"e5d16018-2020-41b2-88f2-80c18c9d8446","uuid":"e0f7d94b-6112-475b-804f-830a347c79d1"}
